WEBVTT

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:33.080
Today I'm on a journey through Devon.

00:00:32.159 --> 00:00:39.760
Starting here in the waters near Thurleston and heading all the way east to Tiverton.

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:50.640
My travelling will take me from Thurleston to Plympton, where I'll meet a man who bought his own zoo.

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:55.320
At Calstock, I'll take a trip down the River Tamar, to see a working water mill at St Dominick.

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:58.760
Finally, at Tiverton, I'll be saddling up and trying my hand at an ancient marshal art.

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:08.320
Along the way, I'll be taking a look back at the best of the BBC's rural programmes from this part of the UK.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:20.400
Devon is a captivating mix of high moors, historic town, wind-blown estuaries and coastal grandeur.

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:26.080
It contains the two National Parks of Exmoor and Dartmoor.

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:34.360
It is the only county in England to have two separate coastlines - the Bristol Channel in the north and the English Channel to the south.

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:34.680
It's on the south-west coast where my travels begin today.

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:36.600
Swimming here is a real treat.

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:39.440
Such a great way to get the day started.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:45.480
But in these waters, I've been told, I could be just above a shipwreck.

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:53.560
The entire coast of the British Isles is a ship graveyard.

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:54.480
Vessels from centuries of sailing are strewn across the sea floor.

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:58.320
South Devon's coast is no exception.

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:04.000
Scattered along its entire lengths are dozens of shipwrecks, pulverised and then hidden by the sea.

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:18.720
Somewhere below me lies the remains of the Louis Sheid, a Belgium steamer that sunk in 1939, but before I explore the wreck I've got to get kitted out.

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:25.760
I've teamed up with dive instructor Kieran Hatton, who is going to teach me how to snorkel in open water.

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:33.120
So, I've been in, and it's absolutely freezing.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:35.280
Is the weather conducive for snorkelling today?

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:35.880
For sure.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:42.160
We are really, really lucky today with a light offshore breeze and dead flat sea conditions.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:43.520
We can see from the boat that the water's nice and clear, really the pick of the year, so far.

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:48.160
Is this one of the best areas to snorkel in across this coast?

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:51.120
Well, marine life wise it is very diverse.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:03:04.840
As a coastline it is a jagged changing coastline, which provides a lot of environment for marine life to grow on, but also I'm interested in the history, the shipwrecks, the Louis Sheid that we have got off the rocks here, the story behind how they got there, why they got there, what they were doing before they got there.

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:09.040
So when we snorkel above the Louis Sheid, what are we going to see?

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:15.280
We are going to jump on the main body of the wreck, over what would be the engine room, the boiler.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:18.480
They are three really big obvious reference points.

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:20.320
They are standing about four metres proud of the seabed.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:27.720
This being early in the season, is a great time, the kelp has not grown really tall, so we will see the main body of the wreck.

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:36.040
The best bit is if you look for stuff this big, you won't miss anything in the sea if you swim around looking for your big...

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:38.360
I'm not going to see any killer whales?

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:44.960
No man-eating things, but if we're really lucky we could get big basking sharks, come along the coast here.

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:47.160
They are filter feeders, they swim along the surface.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:49.520
Their fin and the tip of their nose, giving the tell-tale as to where they are.

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:50.800
SINGS "Jaws" THEME TUNE Same family.

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:52.080
Not man-eaters, though?

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:52.640
No.

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:57.960
So, we'll be OK?

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:38.720
It may just look like a lot of seaweed, but when you delve a little deeper, the remains of the Louis Sheid become apparent.

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:51.640
The engine room... ..and the hull are clearly visible, but if you didn't know it was here, there's a really good chance you could swim right over it.

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:04.080
Why was it wrecked here?

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:05.880
It's very unfortunate.

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:15.480
She went to the aid of a Dutch steam ship that had been torpedoed out in the channel and she collected the surviving crew from that ship.

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:30.800
Then to protect herself, was running for the safety of the shoreline of the UK, but due to blackout conditions, all of the village lights were blacked out, there were no lighthouses and in a heavy storm, and rain, she narrowly missed the headland and ran straight up the rocks where we are now.

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:31.760
Everyone was taken off, survived?

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:35.880
Yeah, everybody on board the Louis Sheid survived.

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:36.120
Incredible.

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:55.440
The Louis Sheid has been lying here for over 70 years.

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:59.080
Marine life now covers every inch of her.

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:02.560
It's hard to imagine that once she was an ocean-going ship.

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:12.760
What an amazing experience!

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:23.160
Snorkelling with Kieran has given me such an incredible appreciation of Devon from the sea, but now it's time to take my journey inland.

00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:32.280
Thurleston is just one of the many wonderful beaches on this stretch of coast.

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:39.680
Just a few miles away is Carlyon Bay, a beach where all is not what it seems.

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:42.200
You are probably not going to find another beach like this in the world.

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:47.680
Glynda Easterbrook has been examining its extraordinary make-up.

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:51.080
If I brought my children down on to this beach I wouldn't give this stuff a second thought.

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:51.920
It looks like sand.

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:53.360
It does, doesn't it.

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:59.480
But in this bottle, I have some sand that I collected from another beach somewhere else in Cornwall.

00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:01.320
If you look at it closely, you can see how different it is.

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:03.400
This is very soft, almost smooth.

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:04.440
This is hard and granular.

00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:10.600
It's nice and soft because it has shell-y fragments in it.

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:13.960
This is nearly all quartz.

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:18.480
This is no average beach.

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:25.320
All other Cornish beaches are a mixture of eroded granite and crushed sea shells, but this one at Carlyon Bay is mostly made of quartz.

00:07:25.640 --> 00:07:29.120
This is a typical Cornish granite.

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:35.640
Granite is made of three main minerals - quartz, which is this grey glassy stuff here.

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:42.240
It's got great big white crystals of a mineral that we call feldspar and it's got some little black shiny grains which is mica.

00:07:41.320 --> 00:07:45.600
The quartz and the mica ended up on the beach, but where did the feldspar go?

00:07:44.680 --> 00:07:45.880
That's the secret to this beach.

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:59.240
The feldspar has been removed, it's been extracted by the china clay industry, but they've been left with the quartz and it's now found its way down on to this beach.

00:07:58.360 --> 00:08:00.480
This beach is a strip of industrial garbage?

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:00.760
That's right.

00:07:59.960 --> 00:08:05.840
This beautiful beach is actually the waste product left over by the extraction of china clay from granite.

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:16.480
The mica and quartz that make up this unusual beach have been washed down river from the clay processing plants inland.

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:22.880
These huge pits are where the clay is extracted from the granite, generating a waste sand that has accumulated at Carlyon Bay.

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:31.600
China clay might get its name from the Chinese potters that made it famous, but one quarter of the world's china clay comes from Cornish granite.

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:38.200
As the rock weathers over millions of years, it softens, making this rotten granite ripe for mining.

00:08:38.559 --> 00:08:48.000
That high pressure hose is blasting geologically rotten granite into a slurry that can be processed into china clay.

00:08:52.480 --> 00:09:02.920
Once you have the clay, which is the mineral component it needs more processing before it becomes china clay.

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:03.640
These pits produce two million tonnes a year.

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:06.600
That's a lot of tea cups!

00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:07.120
But this is about much more than pottery.

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:11.040
Do a little research and you find china clay is in lots of things.

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:15.560
Bizarrely, it has over 500 different commercial uses, some of them quite unexpected!

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:25.200
So, what exactly is china clay?

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:29.080
In simple terms it's rotten granite.

00:09:28.280 --> 00:09:36.120
In fact the white material in there has a whole range of uses, everything from manufacturing paper to paint.

00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:42.320
So, why do you need this stuff, rather than any other raw material to put it into car dash boards, or paper or paint or tooth paste or pills or whatever?

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:50.720
It's a hugely versatile mineral which can give gloss to paper...

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:52.920
Oh, the shine to paper?

00:09:52.120 --> 00:09:57.320
It can give shine to paper, it can give impact resistance in a fender in a car.

00:09:56.480 --> 00:10:03.400
It can be used in the electrical installation wiring systems in a dash board, so there are many, many uses.

00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:09.080
Of course, many of us will take china clay in the form of medicine.

00:10:08.200 --> 00:10:10.880
Why would I want to put this in some medicine?

00:10:10.760 --> 00:10:13.600
Simply, it is an inert carrier.

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:15.760
It carries the other chemicals through the system.

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:16.160
So it's a fluid?

00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:17.640
Just to carry it through the body, yes.

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:22.560
Which of the various products that I've brought along has the most china clay in it?

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:25.920
Well, surprisingly it is paper.

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:32.760
A magazine like the Radio Times would contain a third by weight of china clay.

00:10:34.880 --> 00:10:41.880
For every one tonne of china clay extracted, there are nine tonnes of waste.

00:10:41.240 --> 00:10:45.760
So spectacular were the mountains of waste, that they were known locally as the Cornish Alps.

00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:54.520
In the last decade, the industry's been trying to clean up its act, by landscaping these man-made hills.

00:10:53.560 --> 00:11:00.640
One disused china clay pit has been transformed into a tourist attraction, the Eden Project.

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:04.040
And a new use has been found for the waste.

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:06.560
It is turned into aggregates.

00:11:05.840 --> 00:11:14.200
This means there will be no new sand washed down to Carlyon Bay so, the beach will not be replenished.

00:11:13.240 --> 00:11:20.360
While the developers and the locals debate the future of this beach, they are actually losing it.

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:31.120
The china clay industry created the beach, property developers want to capitalise on it, but nature could snatch it away far faster than many people realise.

00:11:32.560 --> 00:11:39.960
It's four years since Nick visited Carlyon Bay, but development of the site still has not begun.

00:11:39.160 --> 00:11:41.720
I'm on a journey through Devon.

00:11:41.480 --> 00:11:49.240
I've left Thurleston and the coast behind me and travel to Plympton to pay a visit to Dartmoor Zoo.

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:55.680
In October, 2006, Ben Mee and his family bought Dartmoor Zoo.

00:11:54.760 --> 00:12:05.040
The zoo was in serious neglect and was in danger of being shut down permanently, but thankfully for the animals and for the zoo, Ben Mee and his family stepped in.

00:12:04.280 --> 00:12:06.720
They bought it.

00:12:06.920 --> 00:12:11.320
This created a lot of media interest at the time.

00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:13.920
The zoo became the subject of a BBC TV series.

00:12:18.360 --> 00:12:26.920
Ben Mee, writer and animal lover has taken on the challenge of his life.

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:33.400
With no previous experience, Ben, his mother and brother have sold the family home.

00:12:32.520 --> 00:12:35.560
And bought a zoo.

00:12:38.920 --> 00:12:44.520
Everyone tells us this is an enormous risk, friends, family, lawyers, bankers, accountants, we know it is a big risk, don't we?

00:12:43.560 --> 00:12:44.680
But...

00:12:44.880 --> 00:12:46.040
We do.

00:12:45.080 --> 00:12:46.760
But it's one we want to take.

00:12:45.960 --> 00:12:55.440
It used to be a top tourist attraction, but now, closed after council inspections, it's a zoo on death row.

00:12:56.720 --> 00:13:05.280
If you have not got this licence renewed, the animals will have to be dispersed or shot.

00:13:04.400 --> 00:13:09.520
Can Ben and his family save the animals and reopen the zoo?

00:13:09.520 --> 00:13:14.800
I've caught up with Ben to see how zoo life has been treating him these past four years.

00:13:15.960 --> 00:13:20.200
So, it's been four years on, how have things changed?

00:13:21.080 --> 00:13:23.800
Well, this is a good example of one of the things we did earlier.

00:13:23.320 --> 00:13:30.760
This used to be a very grim... puma enclosure with two very elderly pumas.

00:13:29.960 --> 00:13:34.320
Where we are standing now was thick laurel, it was very dark.

00:13:33.440 --> 00:13:41.080
We have cleared that away and increased the size of it and got a breeding pair of lynx in here, and they have actually been mating.

00:13:40.200 --> 00:13:44.960
So we may get some little cubs.

00:13:44.280 --> 00:13:50.360
You came with zero experience with, you know, big cats and all these animals.

00:13:49.440 --> 00:13:51.000
How much have you learned?

00:13:50.800 --> 00:13:56.480
I've learned a lot in a very short time and I'm still really at the bottom of a very steep learning curve.

00:13:56.800 --> 00:14:07.640
But we established early on if we were going to do it, you have to employ proper professional keepers and a curator from whom you take your instructions.

00:14:06.840 --> 00:14:14.640
If he advises me that I need to call the vet to treat the lion's cyst, I do.

00:14:13.880 --> 00:14:17.280
I don't say, "It will be fine.

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:17.680
I'm the director."

00:14:16.840 --> 00:14:19.920
You listen to the staff.

00:14:20.280 --> 00:14:23.320
And in that sense we are completely in the hands of the professionals.

00:14:22.520 --> 00:14:28.320
My job, really, is to lie awake at night, worrying about money.

00:14:28.400 --> 00:14:30.000
Oh, no!

00:14:29.160 --> 00:14:35.480
Everyone else, even the keepers and the people that work here has a really nice time, except me!

00:14:34.720 --> 00:14:37.440
They are saying, "What a great place".

00:14:36.520 --> 00:14:37.680
I'm thinking, "Is it?"

00:14:37.920 --> 00:14:41.560
But I do hear there might be a light at the end of the tunnel.

00:14:40.600 --> 00:14:49.160
A little birdie has told me that Hollywood might be interested in your story?

00:14:48.200 --> 00:14:58.000
Astonishingly, when I wrote a book a while ago about the whole thing, they sold the film rights and this is fairly routine and often nothing happens.

00:14:57.200 --> 00:15:03.160
Usually they shelve those projects and sit on them so no-one else can make them.

00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:09.560
But I got a call the other day from Hollywood, it was late at night, on the phone, an unknown.

00:15:09.160 --> 00:15:13.000
I'm thinking, "Do I want to take an unknown call at 10.30pm?"

00:15:12.080 --> 00:15:17.440
It was "Hi, Ben, it's Julie from Hollywood."

00:15:16.880 --> 00:15:22.240
"I'd just like to tell you we have a release date for Christmas 2011."

00:15:21.640 --> 00:15:28.800
There are all these rumours of Matt Damon or Ben Stiller playing me.

00:15:27.920 --> 00:15:32.320
It can't really be Matt Damon or Ben Stiller.

00:15:31.360 --> 00:15:32.680
She is saying, "They are not my first choice.

00:15:31.720 --> 00:15:35.920
"We have all the A-listers."

00:15:34.960 --> 00:15:36.080
I'm thinking, "Yeah, yeah, sure."

00:15:35.280 --> 00:15:37.520
Yes, probably a prank call, prank call?!

00:15:36.600 --> 00:15:43.320
Unknown, this is probably my sister winding me up.

00:15:42.440 --> 00:15:47.280
But they are going to set it in Hollywood.

00:15:46.440 --> 00:15:52.520
Recreate all of these enclosures in a lot as it is cheaper than coming here and filming.

00:15:51.720 --> 00:15:55.040
I have to go over and advise, obviously, they did say.

00:15:54.360 --> 00:15:57.720
So, fingers crossed, Hollywood.

00:15:56.920 --> 00:15:59.200
Yes, poorly finger crossed.

00:15:58.520 --> 00:15:59.560
Come on, Hollywood.

00:16:01.520 --> 00:16:02.760
Gosh, that would be amazing.

00:16:02.040 --> 00:16:05.400
Now, can you hear that?

00:16:04.760 --> 00:16:09.360
That is Solomon calling for his lunch.

00:16:08.480 --> 00:16:10.000
GROWLING Shall we sort him out?

00:16:09.240 --> 00:16:10.680
For his lunch?

00:16:09.720 --> 00:16:11.400
Hm-mm.

00:16:10.720 --> 00:16:12.720
OK.

00:16:11.920 --> 00:16:14.040
Are you up for a lion feed?

00:16:14.440 --> 00:16:16.280
I'll watch.

00:16:17.040 --> 00:16:19.320
Oh, he's hungry!

00:16:19.880 --> 00:16:23.320
Have I got to sacrifice myself?

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:25.080
Well, it can get quite dangerous.

00:16:25.360 --> 00:16:28.520
Ben is definitely chucking me in at the deep end here.

00:16:27.600 --> 00:16:35.720
Of course, if feeding lions does not grab you, there are plenty of other more relaxing ways to pass your time in this part of the world.

00:16:35.040 --> 00:16:36.440
As Miriam O'Reilly discovered.

00:16:51.680 --> 00:16:58.960
Back in the 1930s if you wanted to be in the swim, then you visited a lido.

00:16:58.080 --> 00:17:07.760
A new wave of pleasure seekers looking for something a little more continental and stylish, flocked to these elegant new pools with their flamboyant, modern architecture.

00:17:09.839 --> 00:17:14.839
In the 1930s, 170 lidos were built in Britain.

00:17:14.040 --> 00:17:17.359
Now just a few, like Tinside in Plymouth, remain.

00:17:17.079 --> 00:17:24.520
Journalist and keen swimmer Janet Smith has traced the history of the lidos from their earliest days.

00:17:24.839 --> 00:17:34.080
The origins of the word "lido" is from the Italian and the very fashionable beach they had at Venice, known as the Lido.

00:17:33.240 --> 00:17:36.080
So the word was adopted from there.

00:17:35.360 --> 00:17:41.480
I'm calling them LEE-dos, you are calling them LIE-dos, which are they, are they LEE-dos or LIE-dos?

00:17:41.040 --> 00:17:44.040
This is a very good question.

00:17:43.360 --> 00:17:46.840
I'm sure you will hear both of them as you travel around.

00:17:46.080 --> 00:17:52.640
A lot of people do say LIE-do and I'm one of them.

00:17:51.760 --> 00:17:57.520
Correctly, it's LEE-do because it's Italian, but it's become anglicised.

00:17:56.760 --> 00:17:59.640
There are other Italian words we anglicise.

00:17:59.040 --> 00:18:04.520
Cinema is really an Italian word, but who says CHIN-ema?

00:18:03.560 --> 00:18:05.480
So why not anglicise lido as well?

00:18:04.560 --> 00:18:07.360
Why was it that lidos were adopted by the British?

00:18:08.320 --> 00:18:12.360
Knowing our weather and how changeable it can be?

00:18:11.480 --> 00:18:16.920
Funnily enough they did already exist here from the turn of the century, there were very basic open-air pools.

00:18:16.120 --> 00:18:26.760
But in the 1930s they really came over in a big way from Germany, where they were very into open-air swimming and open-air exercise, generally.

00:18:25.880 --> 00:18:31.320
It was a very simple way for people to enjoy, almost a holiday.

00:18:31.880 --> 00:18:33.080
It was like a holiday destination.

00:18:32.120 --> 00:18:46.200
Something that could be done very cheaply and very easily and by the whole family and you didn't have to travel very far to get to your nearest pool because nearly every town and city throughout the country would have its own lido.

00:18:51.280 --> 00:19:08.680
Tinside Lido is thriving today, but back in the early '90s, it fell on hard times and was almost derelict, but when the council announced plans to demolish it, local people rallied and the pool was saved.

00:19:07.920 --> 00:19:10.200
Tinside is now enjoyed by visitors from around the world.

00:19:09.520 --> 00:19:12.040
This is actually the first time we've come.

00:19:11.200 --> 00:19:13.760
My wife has a memory of this.

00:19:13.160 --> 00:19:20.960
She used to come here as a child on holiday when her parents lived in Hampshire.

00:19:20.120 --> 00:19:23.000
It was her regular holiday spot.

00:19:23.400 --> 00:19:26.520
So we've come here to find it exactly, it must have been as it was before.

00:19:28.960 --> 00:19:31.560
It was lovely to find it put to use again.

00:19:30.640 --> 00:19:34.640
Because as a child, you know, we used to come up here most of the time.

00:19:33.840 --> 00:19:37.080
It is even lovelier now, really, than it ever used to be.

00:19:40.160 --> 00:19:45.080
I have never seen a pool like this before, it is beautiful.

00:19:44.200 --> 00:19:50.760
And when I jumped in, not realising that it was a saltwater pool it was a bit of a shock!

00:19:49.880 --> 00:19:53.200
And it was a bit cold too, but it's lovely once you get used to it!

00:20:07.360 --> 00:20:08.760
Once you're in your swimming kit, that is a great leveller so they were really for all classes of people.

00:20:07.800 --> 00:20:10.480
I don't think there was any great snobbery about it.

00:20:09.840 --> 00:20:17.440
People liked to go to their lido and enjoy themselves, so all classes would very much mix together at the pool.

00:20:16.600 --> 00:20:27.800
Whereas the indoor pools, the earlier indoor pools, there would be first and second-class swimming baths under the same roof, but that never happened at the lidos.

00:20:26.960 --> 00:20:30.640
It was one pool for all.

00:20:42.600 --> 00:20:46.200
No such relaxation for me.

00:20:45.600 --> 00:20:50.960
I'm at Dartmoor Zoo and it's feeding time for the lions.

00:20:50.520 --> 00:20:56.080
Now, Colin is luring them into the house with a little bit of food.

00:20:55.200 --> 00:21:04.160
That will enable us to lock them into the house, go into the enclosure and we distribute the meat in as interesting places as we can think of.

00:21:16.080 --> 00:21:18.520
That noise, feel it.

00:21:17.760 --> 00:21:19.320
Hello, big fellow.

00:21:31.560 --> 00:21:33.080
He is just... incredible!

00:21:32.200 --> 00:21:34.520
God, he nearly gave me a heart attack!

00:21:42.560 --> 00:21:46.280
Look into his eyes, they're really...

00:21:46.160 --> 00:21:47.440
Lacking in sympathy.

00:21:46.880 --> 00:21:48.560
Yes!

00:21:47.800 --> 00:21:49.360
He's looking at the outside, thinking that's where food's coming.

00:21:48.680 --> 00:21:53.720
He knows the ritual, this is all a lot of show.

00:21:53.080 --> 00:21:54.400
That little wire...

00:21:53.560 --> 00:21:56.760
That little bit of mesh.

00:21:56.400 --> 00:21:58.680
That will keep that from...?

00:21:59.800 --> 00:22:02.200
It usually holds!

00:22:01.360 --> 00:22:02.720
Usually.

00:22:01.800 --> 00:22:03.160
Yep!

00:22:02.520 --> 00:22:04.920
It should be all right.

00:22:04.080 --> 00:22:08.440
Solomon, you be a good boy, now... when I'm walking around your enclosure with lumps of meat.

00:22:07.480 --> 00:22:09.400
Right.

00:22:16.320 --> 00:22:17.880
We're going in.

00:22:17.000 --> 00:22:20.200
So, what are we feeding them?

00:22:19.360 --> 00:22:22.120
This is pony.

00:22:25.480 --> 00:22:32.600
'Ben tells me that Josie the female lion likes to climb for her food.

00:22:34.760 --> 00:22:38.080
They can get up this high?

00:22:37.120 --> 00:22:38.400
Yeah.

00:22:38.320 --> 00:22:39.440
Amazing.

00:22:38.480 --> 00:22:40.960
You can see the claw marks on the trunks.

00:22:42.440 --> 00:22:44.440
Look at him, he's looking at me.

00:22:45.200 --> 00:22:48.560
I tell you what, if anything happens I'm going so far up this tree.

00:22:55.840 --> 00:23:03.840
Although they're spoon-fed like this and have been all their lives, I think they're third generation captive-bred, they still have the basic instincts that they need in the wild.

00:23:03.560 --> 00:23:11.080
A couple of Christmases ago, we made a zebra out of cardboard, painted it up, put bits of meat in it and stuff.

00:23:10.160 --> 00:23:13.320
Put it down there with the lions locked in.

00:23:12.600 --> 00:23:17.960
I thought maybe they'll be scared, seeing this huge thing.

00:23:17.120 --> 00:23:18.840
It'd be embarrassing, cos people would be taking pictures.

00:23:18.280 --> 00:23:22.920
We opened the hatch and it was four seconds between the hatch and the zebra.

00:23:22.200 --> 00:23:23.360
The lioness was on the back, dragged it down.

00:23:30.160 --> 00:23:37.160
When the kill was confirmed, Solomon swanned up and took the carcass, stood over it and bellowed for four days.

00:23:36.320 --> 00:23:37.880
"My kill."

00:23:37.760 --> 00:23:39.120
And she's going, "I killed it, I killed it!"

00:23:38.840 --> 00:23:42.440
She is the real killer and he is the showman.

00:23:42.760 --> 00:23:47.760
So if they took four seconds to get from there, all the way down there...

00:23:46.960 --> 00:23:48.280
Yes!

00:23:47.360 --> 00:23:52.040
He's staring at me right now from there, so he can get to me in a split second.

00:23:51.840 --> 00:23:53.920
Yes, less than one second.

00:23:53.720 --> 00:23:57.280
I think it's time to go.

00:23:56.360 --> 00:23:58.760
I'm out, I'm alive!

00:24:34.520 --> 00:24:36.040
Oh!

00:24:35.240 --> 00:24:37.640
Amazing.

00:24:36.800 --> 00:24:39.640
Well, when she climbed that tree, I was glad I wasn't still up there.

00:24:39.760 --> 00:24:43.200
It's a strange place to be in there, isn't it?

00:24:42.400 --> 00:24:44.800
Once you're in there, you realise, that's your five-minute window.

00:24:43.920 --> 00:24:49.400
If you were in there now, they'd be eating us.

00:24:48.960 --> 00:24:54.400
You've had the zoo three years now.

00:24:53.440 --> 00:24:57.360
Is this why you did it - the adrenaline rush.

00:24:56.400 --> 00:24:58.440
Walking in, seeing it, feeding them.

00:24:57.560 --> 00:25:01.080
For me, the first time, I'm just blown away.

00:25:02.320 --> 00:25:07.920
Well, it is a really very happy by-product of... owning it and living here.

00:25:07.000 --> 00:25:09.280
That you get to do this.

00:25:08.480 --> 00:25:12.880
Although I don't get to do it that much, as I'm usually stuck at a desk somewhere.

00:25:11.920 --> 00:25:13.320
Right.

00:25:12.400 --> 00:25:15.640
Messing about with accountants and things.

00:25:14.800 --> 00:25:20.760
I know running a zoo is hard work, and I know it's been tough for the last couple of years for you.

00:25:20.280 --> 00:25:22.040
Would you do it again?

00:25:22.240 --> 00:25:24.280
I used to have an answer for that, which was, "Absolutely.

00:25:24.000 --> 00:25:25.120
No question."

00:25:24.200 --> 00:25:27.200
And after this winter I'm thinking...

00:25:26.760 --> 00:25:29.320
But no, I totally would.

00:25:28.520 --> 00:25:34.040
There's something about this place, it has its own energy, momentum.

00:25:33.080 --> 00:25:34.800
All these animals here.

00:25:34.400 --> 00:25:38.640
The way people react when they come and see them.

00:25:37.720 --> 00:25:41.160
Definitely, I'd do it again, yes.

00:25:42.200 --> 00:25:47.480
That was the most exhilarating experience I have ever had.

00:25:46.520 --> 00:25:49.080
Just the power.

00:25:48.200 --> 00:25:52.840
And when he bellowed you could feel it in your stomach.

00:25:53.600 --> 00:25:55.560
That's something I'll never, ever going to forget.

00:25:58.440 --> 00:26:03.480
Sadly it is time to leave Dartmoor Zoo and continue on my Devon journey.

00:26:03.080 --> 00:26:09.360
When Matt Baker and Julia Bradbury visited Devon, they went to explore the picturesque Tamar Valley.

00:26:09.720 --> 00:26:18.160
But this area isn't just famous for its landscape, there used to be a thriving mining industry here, digging out copper, tin and arsenic.

00:26:17.240 --> 00:26:21.720
Arsenic has been used as a pesticide and an insecticide.

00:26:21.280 --> 00:26:27.160
It's extremely poisonous, and residues have been abandoned across the county, resulting in spoil heaps like these.

00:26:28.320 --> 00:26:35.560
It might look a little unsightly to some, but to a mountain biker, they offer the same adrenaline-pumping terrain that bikers love.

00:26:35.960 --> 00:26:41.440
Now that used to be a problem, but thanks to a series of new trails, they can cycle in safety.

00:26:42.600 --> 00:26:48.640
Tim, what were your major concerns about bikers biking through this terrain?

00:26:47.680 --> 00:26:51.920
Well, we're in one of the most contaminated sites in Britain.

00:26:51.480 --> 00:26:57.960
The mine was the biggest copper and biggest arsenic producer in the world in the mid-19th century.

00:26:57.880 --> 00:26:58.920
This on the walls behind us here, that is arsenic, isn't it?

00:26:58.120 --> 00:27:00.960
That is some of the remnants of arsenic.

00:27:00.560 --> 00:27:12.360
The bikers that came here knew that if they scraped their knee on it, it would take some time to heal, so they were well aware of the dangers, but also well aware of the excitement they had on the site as well.

00:27:11.640 --> 00:27:16.000
But I'm still seeing bikers around here.

00:27:15.320 --> 00:27:16.640
They are still thrill-seeking?

00:27:15.880 --> 00:27:21.480
Yes, but we've got them off the worst sites onto safer routes.

00:27:20.800 --> 00:27:28.280
So we've built a big infrastructure so they can get in here safely, so they're organised, insured and safe.

00:27:36.080 --> 00:27:38.360
Goodness me!

00:27:37.600 --> 00:27:42.400
The Woodland Riders are the biggest downhill mountain bike club in the country.

00:27:41.680 --> 00:27:43.240
They're based here in the Tamar Valley, and as you can see, these boys don't mess about!

00:27:53.240 --> 00:28:04.400
These new downhill tracks came about through co-operation with the riders, the Tamar Valley AONB and a big chunk of Lottery funding.

00:28:03.560 --> 00:28:08.760
So, Derek, this then is a great example of reuse of an old industrial place?

00:28:08.160 --> 00:28:15.480
It's a fabulous old mining area, which we're now using effectively for an alternative use, which everybody's loving.

00:28:14.840 --> 00:28:19.000
We saw the possibilities over here and had the support to make it possible here.

00:28:18.920 --> 00:28:25.360
Three new bike tracks have been built here, dropping 500 feet in less than a mile.

00:28:26.440 --> 00:28:30.720
So, Rowan, where do you start when you design a course like this?

00:28:31.400 --> 00:28:37.680
We're looking for exactly this - the natural features on the hill.

00:28:36.960 --> 00:28:44.040
Is it difficult when you're working in an Area of Outstanding Natural Beauty, are there constraints there?

00:28:43.520 --> 00:28:45.160
It throws up a few challenges which you perhaps wouldn't have elsewhere.

00:28:44.880 --> 00:28:49.720
If you had a normal plantation, you can pretty much do what you like, blank canvass.

00:28:48.960 --> 00:28:54.920
But on this site, there are some archaeological constraints.

00:28:53.960 --> 00:28:57.400
That set some boundaries for us.

00:28:57.520 --> 00:29:02.840
When you're looking at an area like this, do you give certain routes through it, easy options?

00:29:02.120 --> 00:29:10.600
Although this track we're on now is aimed at more advanced riders, there are still different options and lines.

00:29:09.760 --> 00:29:16.480
So this one we're looking at here is the more expert line, because it takes more skill to ride, and there's an easier, gentler line around the back.

00:29:20.920 --> 00:29:26.320
'Well, now I'm fully clued up about the course, 'it's time to get on my bike and put on plenty of safety gear.'

00:29:27.600 --> 00:29:32.800
So, Rowan, we're tackling the HSD course, which means?

00:29:31.920 --> 00:29:34.080
High Speed Descent.

00:29:33.840 --> 00:29:37.840
Right, and how fast is that?

00:30:07.440 --> 00:30:10.160
Wow!

00:30:09.560 --> 00:30:14.200
Tell you what, talk about high-speed descent, you don't half get some speed up down there.

00:30:13.240 --> 00:30:21.520
But this is your first time back here since the official opening, as the designer, it must be brilliant to see people enjoying this course.

00:30:20.600 --> 00:30:21.760
Yeah.

00:30:20.840 --> 00:30:24.520
You've a grin on your face.

00:30:23.560 --> 00:30:24.680
I have!

00:30:23.760 --> 00:30:28.200
I enjoy it, I know I'm going to, but it's good to see other people enjoying it.

00:30:27.360 --> 00:30:28.680
Matt Baker getting to grips with some extreme mountain biking.

00:30:35.000 --> 00:30:39.440
My journey has now taken me to Calstock.

00:30:38.600 --> 00:30:45.160
It's a charming village near the Tamar Valley, but I can't stay long, I need to catch a ride on the Tamar Valley ferry.

00:30:50.800 --> 00:30:57.400
I've been told that travelling on the Tamar Valley ferry is like stepping back in time.

00:30:56.560 --> 00:31:04.880
We're heading downstream to Cotehele, it's only about a 15-minute journey and it allows me to take in the most beautiful river scenery.

00:31:17.800 --> 00:31:24.080
The Tamar Valley is a classified Area Of Outstanding Natural Beauty.

00:31:23.520 --> 00:31:27.320
It also forms a part of the border between Devon and Cornwall.

00:31:26.360 --> 00:31:27.520
What a wonderful way to travel!

00:31:32.480 --> 00:31:37.000
I've arrived so relaxed at my next destination, Cotehele National Park.

00:31:36.200 --> 00:31:39.000
The National Park is also home to a beautiful stately home.

00:31:44.720 --> 00:31:54.280
High above the wooded valley of the Tamar River there stands a house which has been on this site for nearly six centuries - Cotehele.

00:31:54.880 --> 00:32:05.360
And until it was acquired by the National Trust in 1947, it had been the home of just one family, the Edgcumbes.

00:32:13.560 --> 00:32:21.840
It's one of the jewels of the National Trust's treasure box and the job of managing it goes to Rachel Hunt who actually lives in the house.

00:32:20.880 --> 00:32:25.800
What's it like, not just working, but living in a house like this?

00:32:25.680 --> 00:32:27.280
Well, it's really lovely.

00:32:26.480 --> 00:32:31.120
In any other job, you wouldn't get the chance to live somewhere like this, so it is a privilege, but a mixed blessing, you take the rough with the smooth.

00:32:30.240 --> 00:32:36.320
If the alarm goes off in the night, then it becomes your problem.

00:32:35.480 --> 00:32:36.680
So you're living above the shop, really?

00:32:35.760 --> 00:32:38.120
Yes.

00:32:37.200 --> 00:32:42.280
It doesn't look to have changed all that much for hundreds of years.

00:32:43.200 --> 00:32:51.800
Well, it's quite deceptive, because it was actually remodelled in 1862, the butler's house back there was completely added on, so it has changed quite a lot.

00:32:52.160 --> 00:32:55.200
What about the inside?

00:32:54.240 --> 00:33:03.760
Some remodelling was carried out in the 1650s, but it's remained without electric lighting and only very limited electricity.

00:33:02.880 --> 00:33:04.080
Really?

00:33:03.600 --> 00:33:04.840
Yes.

00:33:03.960 --> 00:33:06.080
Why is that?

00:33:05.200 --> 00:33:11.920
Well, to install electric lighting and major electrical works would compromise the integrity of the building.

00:33:11.520 --> 00:33:15.640
It's got a lovely character as it is, and part of the character is the gloominess.

00:33:18.280 --> 00:33:25.720
Cotehele is just one of around 300 properties looked after by the National Trust, ranging from lighthouses to castles.

00:33:25.480 --> 00:33:38.360
The Trust was formed in late Victorian times, but it really came into its own in 1907, with an Act Of Parliament which allowed it to keep everything it has for everyone forever.

00:33:41.120 --> 00:33:46.880
Mark Harold has worked for the Trust for 20 years, and now oversees everything it holds in Devon and Cornwall.

00:33:46.400 --> 00:33:48.680
How did the Trust come about?

00:33:47.840 --> 00:33:50.480
It happened at a time when there were lots of different movements looking at the protection of places.

00:33:49.520 --> 00:34:10.520
And it brought together three very special people - Octavia Hill, who was interested in providing breathing spaces for the poor, spaces where they could get some form of recreation, and Canon Hardwicke Rawnsley and also Robert Hunter.

00:34:09.679 --> 00:34:13.400
Those three special people actually came together to form the basis of the National Trust.

00:34:15.480 --> 00:34:18.040
Did the idea take off straight away?

00:34:17.280 --> 00:34:27.639
It did, but they were involved in other things too, so the early days were quite slow, but actually collecting together a few special properties.

00:34:27.000 --> 00:34:42.760
In fact, some of our very early attempts to protect places, like Sayes Court Garden, weren't successful, but they gave that sort of passion and that desire to protect, which really has grown and grown in the Trust that we see today.

00:34:43.400 --> 00:34:49.239
Of course, the big thing that boosted the Trust was the act that more or less created it in 1907?

00:34:49.400 --> 00:35:01.120
Particularly that part which actually set out our core thing, the one thing that really makes us special, and that's our ability to declare land inalienable.

00:35:00.240 --> 00:35:06.680
And what that means, really, is that we hold things forever and they're protected forever.

00:35:05.840 --> 00:35:14.720
So once we've declared something inalienable, we can't sell it, it can't be taken away from us, we can't mortgage it.

00:35:13.800 --> 00:35:16.040
It really sets the Trust for that very long view ahead.

00:35:18.880 --> 00:35:25.720
'The house here is just one of the many treasures this National Park has on offer.'

00:35:25.200 --> 00:35:32.120
And a walk through the woodland, away from Cotehele House, leads you...to another.

00:35:31.640 --> 00:35:37.280
Cotehele Water Mill is a restored late-18th-century working water mill.

00:35:37.000 --> 00:35:41.480
There are only around 30 of these left in the UK today.

00:35:40.760 --> 00:35:44.560
So I'm delighted Roger Varrow has agreed to show me around.

00:35:47.040 --> 00:35:50.200
Can you tell me, how does it all work?

00:35:50.120 --> 00:35:51.360
Sure, yes.

00:35:50.400 --> 00:35:54.840
We store our grain on the floor above.

00:35:54.120 --> 00:35:58.600
The grain comes down this chute here, and just by pulling this out, we can allow the grain into this small hopper.

00:36:03.440 --> 00:36:20.000
And as the millstones are turning here, you can see down here, this is called the shoe, and it's being agitated backwards and forwards, and the grain is falling through there, it goes down between the millstones.

00:36:19.640 --> 00:36:24.040
They go in an opposite direction, is that right?

00:36:23.120 --> 00:36:26.240
It's the top stone that's actually turning.

00:36:25.280 --> 00:36:30.040
The bed stone is the one that's stationary, it's fixed to the floor.

00:36:29.240 --> 00:36:34.520
The grain drops in between the two and then the flour is driven around inside and goes down the chute.

00:36:38.960 --> 00:36:43.600
And at the moment you're making plain flour here?

00:36:42.640 --> 00:36:46.160
No, it's wholemeal flour that we produce.

00:36:45.240 --> 00:36:46.440
OK, lovely.

00:36:45.520 --> 00:36:48.200
What's the difference between making a plain and a wholemeal?

00:36:48.120 --> 00:36:55.200
With a wholemeal flour, everything that's in the grain comes out in the flour.

00:36:54.800 --> 00:36:59.640
We don't take anything out of it, so all the goodness is there.

00:36:59.240 --> 00:37:07.680
We take wholemeal flour and then we can make white flour from it by a process of sieving.

00:37:06.880 --> 00:37:09.000
Ah, so this is it, this is where it comes out.

00:37:20.480 --> 00:37:22.280
Can you explain what's going on behind us?

00:37:22.640 --> 00:37:25.640
Yes, certainly.

00:37:24.920 --> 00:37:28.640
Behind the wall there is the water wheel.

00:37:28.120 --> 00:37:32.560
At the bottom, there's a shaft that runs through the wall.

00:37:31.600 --> 00:37:36.320
The water wheel is mounted on the shaft, the other side.

00:37:35.360 --> 00:37:36.560
So the drive's coming through into the mill.

00:37:40.600 --> 00:37:46.560
What you see in front of you here is a very old-fashioned gearbox.

00:37:46.200 --> 00:37:53.000
The water wheel is turning quite slowly, but we need to turn the millstones much, much faster.

00:37:52.120 --> 00:37:56.200
So there used to be so many mills around the UK, suddenly they all disappeared.

00:37:55.400 --> 00:37:57.000
So, what happened?

00:37:57.400 --> 00:38:08.280
At one time, there were thousands of little mills like this all over the country, but round about the 1900s the roller-mill process was introduced.

00:38:07.480 --> 00:38:13.080
And that was a very efficient way of producing flour from wheat grain.

00:38:12.160 --> 00:38:18.960
They were powered, firstly, by steam engines, and later, by large electric motors.

00:38:26.200 --> 00:38:28.280
They could produce flour much more cheaply than little mills like this, using the traditional method.

00:38:31.640 --> 00:38:39.840
The mill and the house here at Cotehele are examples of the nation's history being beautifully maintained to safeguard them for future generations.

00:38:40.280 --> 00:38:48.400
The same, however, cannot be said of Hallsands, a fishing village on the South Devon Coast, which fell victim to the forces of nature in 1917.

00:38:50.560 --> 00:38:55.120
So, how many houses were there originally in Hallsands?

00:38:55.440 --> 00:39:01.760
At its height, there were 37 houses with 159 people.

00:39:07.600 --> 00:39:12.840
Everybody, kind of, owed part of their living either directly or indirectly to the sea.

00:39:11.960 --> 00:39:20.920
The beach had a shingle ridge which was high enough and protective enough for them to be able to leave the boats on the beach all year round.

00:39:20.000 --> 00:39:23.200
It's entirely disappeared now.

00:39:27.480 --> 00:39:34.040
Where we are standing, here, these were the foundations of the London Inn, the only pub in the village.

00:39:37.520 --> 00:39:41.480
So presumably there wasn't just a sheer drop down here at that point?

00:39:40.520 --> 00:39:46.640
No, the sand and shingle came up to the level of this rock platform.

00:39:49.880 --> 00:39:55.640
Before the storm, there would have been a path leading up to the front of this house.

00:39:54.760 --> 00:39:59.040
Today I can only get to it on the end of a rope.

00:40:13.320 --> 00:40:17.840
I wonder what it must have been like on that terrible night?

00:40:16.880 --> 00:40:28.080
On January the 26th, 1917, the wind and waves had been building up all day, until by nightfall, it was a horrendous storm.

00:40:29.200 --> 00:40:40.360
I've got a tape here with the words of Edith Patey, who was one of the survivors of that dreadful night back in 1917.

00:40:43.560 --> 00:40:48.400
We were counting the waves, every wave seemed to be more heavier than the last one.

00:40:48.240 --> 00:40:54.560
And we were counting nine, and we thought this one will be a bigger one.

00:40:53.680 --> 00:41:04.040
Then it was rolling over the rooftops and breaking over thatched roofing, the boards, you know, the rafters.

00:41:03.200 --> 00:41:07.480
It's the very high waves that does the damage.

00:41:08.080 --> 00:41:17.040
Then we could see the floor slipping away, the coal house slipping away, all of it going down to a huge pit.

00:41:21.800 --> 00:41:29.320
It couldn't be repaired, beyond repair, but you have a little place as a sacred memory.

00:41:32.960 --> 00:41:36.360
The amazing thing about that night was that nobody was killed.

00:41:35.560 --> 00:41:40.280
In fact nobody was even seriously injured, but the village was completely ruined.

00:41:44.280 --> 00:41:53.160
In the past, the village had had its fair share of severe weather and survived, but on this night there were other forces at work which together proved catastrophic.

00:41:56.600 --> 00:42:02.800
Oceanographer Vince May has studied the role played by the bay's geology, in turning bad weather into disaster.

00:42:02.800 --> 00:42:09.480
I still don't understand why Hallsands, in particular, was affected though?

00:42:08.680 --> 00:42:11.720
Why that village out of all of the villages on the coast here?

00:42:14.840 --> 00:42:22.960
It was the combination of high tides, strong winds and a lot of shingle removed by dredging.

00:42:22.160 --> 00:42:25.720
The village's misfortune was its shingle beach.

00:42:26.680 --> 00:42:30.680
Left alone, it provided the village with valuable protection against the wind and waves.

00:42:30.360 --> 00:42:43.240
But shingle was worth a lot of money, particularly when, in 1897, Sir John Jackson's engineering company was given a licence to dredge it up and sell it.

00:42:43.120 --> 00:42:50.760
They took out 6,000 tonnes of shingle from along here.

00:42:49.800 --> 00:42:51.280
It sounds a huge amount.

00:42:50.320 --> 00:42:56.320
It is probably something like 12, 15 times more than the material in the beach at the moment.

00:42:56.760 --> 00:43:06.680
You can see, here, the old village of Hallsands and you can see that the beach goes around in front of the headland, you could have walked around in front of it, you couldn't do that today.

00:43:13.280 --> 00:43:25.400
With British Naval power at its peak, Hallsands shingle really came into its own, a vital ingredient in a huge new building programme underway at the Devonport Naval Base just up the coast.

00:43:25.520 --> 00:43:31.160
It was the glory days of the Empire and nobody was listening to the complaints of a few villagers.

00:43:32.640 --> 00:43:37.760
Commander Charles Crichton knows the story better than anyone.

00:43:37.160 --> 00:43:39.080
Just look at this here.

00:43:38.520 --> 00:43:43.320
This is one of the docks, 700-feet long, largely built of concrete.

00:43:43.000 --> 00:43:48.800
So this is it, this is the concrete, this is where the Hallsands shingle went?

00:43:47.840 --> 00:43:50.560
Absolutely, there were about a million tonnes used.

00:43:49.760 --> 00:43:56.480
Of course, the basic mix of it is sand and gravel, hence all that material was bought in.

00:43:59.800 --> 00:44:09.840
Devonport got its big new docks, and Sir John Jackson made himself a fortune, Hallsands, however, was doomed.

00:44:19.720 --> 00:44:25.640
100 of its inhabitants were driven out forever, their homes and livelihoods smashed.

00:44:24.800 --> 00:44:32.440
Horseback archery is an equestrian martial art with origins going back over 2,000 years to the steppes of Eastern Europe and Asia.

00:44:36.720 --> 00:44:51.440
Horseback archery was originally used for warfare, but it's now being rediscovered and reinvented as a sport.

00:44:51.440 --> 00:44:58.120
It arrived in the UK just a few years ago, so I'm really excited that Hayley Bishop is going to teach me the basics.

00:45:03.080 --> 00:45:05.600
So tell me, how long have you been doing that?

00:45:05.200 --> 00:45:08.000
Since about 2007.

00:45:07.160 --> 00:45:08.600
Right.

00:45:07.640 --> 00:45:09.960
How did you get into it?

00:45:09.000 --> 00:45:21.000
Well, I've been riding since I was about four, and I've done archery for the last seven, eight years, with a search on the internet, I found out that there was such thing as horse archery.

00:45:21.560 --> 00:45:22.760
It's getting very popular.

00:45:21.800 --> 00:45:27.000
It's very popular abroad, and we're promoting it in this country.

00:45:27.360 --> 00:45:30.000
Can I have a go?

00:45:29.080 --> 00:45:32.320
Cos I love horses.

00:45:31.440 --> 00:45:32.720
Yes, most definitely.

00:45:32.040 --> 00:45:33.600
But I've never...

00:45:32.720 --> 00:45:35.200
Arched, is that what you'd call it?

00:45:34.360 --> 00:45:36.240
Done archery.

00:45:35.320 --> 00:45:39.360
I've never done archery in my life, but I can ride.

00:45:39.240 --> 00:45:41.280
OK, that's brilliant.

00:45:40.600 --> 00:45:43.640
I can ride real horses, what's this one?

00:45:44.800 --> 00:45:53.880
This is a wooden horse, we learn on the wooden horse so that you know where the position of the neck is, and where to move your bow and what sort of positions you get into, and then you move on to the horse.

00:45:59.240 --> 00:46:03.200
Yup, and I've got some equipment that you need to wear.

00:46:02.480 --> 00:46:03.720
OK.

00:46:02.920 --> 00:46:04.280
This is an arm guard.

00:46:03.560 --> 00:46:04.720
Yeah.

00:46:03.800 --> 00:46:07.160
I'm assuming you are right-handed?

00:46:08.480 --> 00:46:09.840
Yes, right-handed.

00:46:17.480 --> 00:46:25.520
No, you shoot with three, but it's just one finger that can get hurt.

00:46:29.160 --> 00:46:34.600
Right, this is what you'll be using, this is a bow, a Korean bow.

00:46:33.640 --> 00:46:34.960
A Korean bow?

00:46:34.720 --> 00:46:37.760
It's the style of bow, it's nice and short.

00:46:36.840 --> 00:46:39.720
It's brilliant for horseback archery, it doesn't hit the horse or anything.

00:46:39.680 --> 00:46:45.240
These are three arrows, we generally only use three arrows.

00:47:12.000 --> 00:47:13.120
So one above, two below.

00:47:12.200 --> 00:47:14.280
I've got really long arms!

00:47:14.440 --> 00:47:20.560
You are taking aim from where?

00:47:20.000 --> 00:47:23.360
From this, from your chin?

00:47:22.560 --> 00:47:24.280
No.

00:47:23.400 --> 00:47:30.480
With horse archery you need to be slightly away from your face and you don't actually take aim, it's all instinctive.

00:47:29.640 --> 00:47:32.200
You don't look down the arrow, it's about feel.

00:47:32.840 --> 00:47:34.240
Oh, crikey.

00:47:34.720 --> 00:47:35.880
OK, all right.

00:47:35.080 --> 00:47:36.720
OK?

00:47:35.760 --> 00:47:36.960
Well, shall I give it a go?

00:47:40.160 --> 00:47:42.200
Right, everyone out of the back of shot...

00:47:41.400 --> 00:47:44.560
I don't want to kill anyone!

00:48:00.920 --> 00:48:03.040
I haven't got anywhere near the target!

00:48:02.240 --> 00:48:06.480
Don't worry about that, the whole thing is the target.

00:48:05.880 --> 00:48:12.680
We only use the black as something to aim at, but the whole thing is the target.

00:48:12.000 --> 00:48:13.160
OK.

00:48:12.440 --> 00:48:16.360
Well, I certainly feel like Robin Hood.

00:48:16.720 --> 00:48:19.080
Do you want me to take it a tiny bit harder for you?

00:48:18.480 --> 00:48:20.440
I can hardly do this as it is.

00:48:20.000 --> 00:48:22.120
All right, go on.

00:48:21.240 --> 00:48:29.600
Right, when we are riding the horse, we carry the arrows behind the bow.

00:48:33.440 --> 00:48:35.000
I was expecting to kind of pull them out from my little backpack, like Orlando Bloom in Lord Of The Rings.

00:48:37.400 --> 00:48:43.720
You can do it like that, but it's far easier to have it like that.

00:48:44.160 --> 00:48:46.120
Then you get the arrow, you pull it away, you put it in the bow, and then you push it onto the string and your fingers drop down.

00:49:13.200 --> 00:49:14.240
It's a lot trickier than it looks!

00:49:15.840 --> 00:49:23.040
Right, well I think you are now ready for Woody, our wooden horse.

00:49:35.600 --> 00:49:41.960
Cos it gives you a good feel of where the horse's neck is.

00:49:41.960 --> 00:49:43.560
So you shoot from the side, not in front.

00:49:42.680 --> 00:49:47.360
No, you shoot from the side and from the front and from the back.

00:49:46.560 --> 00:49:48.360
So why do you shoot from all the different angles?

00:49:47.600 --> 00:49:54.400
Actually, it's a Hungarian-style competition.

00:49:53.600 --> 00:50:04.560
They are all from what they would have used in combat, what they found on archaeological digs, that they would have shot forwards, sidewards and backwards.

00:50:17.040 --> 00:50:21.280
Just have to watch how much weight you put over to your right side and keep yourself fairly central.

00:50:30.960 --> 00:50:36.400
I've been riding all my life, would that give me any benefit at all into horse archery?

00:50:36.400 --> 00:50:43.800
It would certainly help with balance and position because you know how to ride, but it doesn't stop beginners from learning.

00:50:43.800 --> 00:50:52.880
As long as they learn the archery safely on the ground and learn how to ride and have balance, they can start doing this from a walk.

00:50:52.040 --> 00:50:54.920
So there is an advantage that you could get on a horse now and learn really quickly.

00:50:54.080 --> 00:50:58.120
Whereas a beginner will take longer, but it doesn't mean that they can't do it.

00:50:58.600 --> 00:51:04.680
But if you can't shoot, you can't shoot!

00:51:04.200 --> 00:51:06.960
No, you definitely have to learn archery.

00:51:09.120 --> 00:51:11.280
So here we go.

00:51:10.560 --> 00:51:12.480
Horse - check.

00:51:11.520 --> 00:51:14.240
Bow and arrow - check.

00:51:13.440 --> 00:51:16.920
Archery skills - not so check.

00:51:16.200 --> 00:51:19.520
Hayley, tell me, what are the rules?

00:51:18.680 --> 00:51:19.840
What have I got to do?

00:51:19.920 --> 00:51:29.640
This is a 90-metre track, and basically you've got to canter down this track, load your arrow to your bow, and take a shot at the centre target.

00:51:28.680 --> 00:51:30.160
The centre one?

00:51:30.480 --> 00:51:33.720
Leave that for now, we could do a front shot, a side shot and a back shot, but now, we'll do the side shot.

00:51:33.000 --> 00:51:34.560
One step at a time.

00:51:33.680 --> 00:51:35.000
Yes!

00:51:34.040 --> 00:51:35.680
I'll go off and I'll try, canter down, bow, arrow, thing, side.

00:51:34.840 --> 00:51:35.920
Got it.

00:51:35.080 --> 00:51:36.360
Right, here we go.

00:51:55.480 --> 00:51:57.720
Well done!

00:51:56.840 --> 00:51:59.360
I meant to get that one(!)

00:51:59.360 --> 00:52:03.800
I definitely meant to get that one(!)

00:52:15.880 --> 00:52:17.560
Well done!

00:52:16.640 --> 00:52:18.200
Centre again!

00:52:17.320 --> 00:52:18.520
Yay!

00:52:17.600 --> 00:52:18.680
Absolutely brilliant.

00:52:22.840 --> 00:52:26.320
Bull's-eye!

00:52:27.480 --> 00:52:28.640
Yeah!

00:52:27.680 --> 00:52:31.840
I was actually aiming for the other one, but don't tell anyone!

00:52:31.360 --> 00:52:33.160
Right, you're doing so well, why not try all three?

00:52:38.200 --> 00:52:41.400
Positive, hold that around there...

00:52:40.480 --> 00:52:42.760
It's the kerfuddling of all the thingies.

00:52:41.960 --> 00:52:43.840
So I have this one already loaded...

00:52:43.480 --> 00:52:44.560
Yep, you can have that one pre-loaded.

00:52:44.280 --> 00:52:45.400
Yep.

00:52:49.160 --> 00:52:54.160
And try to load the others while cantering.

00:52:57.320 --> 00:52:59.040
Oh, I missed it!

00:53:04.320 --> 00:53:07.320
I couldn't load them quick enough!

00:53:06.520 --> 00:53:08.840
That was a little bit harder, that one.

00:53:12.000 --> 00:53:16.600
You've got to learn to load without looking.

00:53:17.360 --> 00:53:20.880
Which is, I think, you know, several years down the line, to be honest.

00:53:35.640 --> 00:53:38.160
Oh, well done!

00:53:37.240 --> 00:53:38.640
Yee-ha!

00:53:41.040 --> 00:53:45.000
You hit the target, that was brilliant!

00:53:44.520 --> 00:53:47.120
Really, really, really loved it.

00:53:46.240 --> 00:53:47.640
Good.

00:53:47.040 --> 00:53:53.160
But I can certainly see how many hours of hard work you've put into it, cos it's a lot more difficult than it looks.

00:53:53.400 --> 00:53:56.240
It is quite difficult, but it's well worth it, isn't it?

00:53:55.400 --> 00:53:57.280
It's brilliant.

00:53:56.600 --> 00:54:01.160
It's such a rush, it really feels great when you get a bull's-eye, which I just did.

00:54:00.200 --> 00:54:01.840
Yay!

00:54:03.760 --> 00:54:09.240
We could do with a good British team, so you never know, we might be ringing you up!

00:54:12.600 --> 00:54:14.160
Here we come, England!

00:54:13.360 --> 00:54:17.200
I started the day snorkelling to find a shipwreck.

00:54:17.240 --> 00:54:20.000
Since then, Devon's provided me with excitement...

00:54:19.440 --> 00:54:22.760
..and adventure.

00:54:21.800 --> 00:54:28.720
So it seems fitting that my journey should end with a new sport that blends archery with horse riding.
